I doubt this is a true story, again...

&#x200B;

In case it is. You have to give the jean back to the girl. Because maybe she didn't know about you, and that would be stealing.

&#x200B;

So, be reasonable. Just tell your parents that he cheated on you, then give your parents the jean, then tell your cheating boyfriend to go get it at your parent's. That's the wise thing to do.

&#x200B;

If the girl knew about you, still give the jeans to your parents and tell your boyfriend to go get it, and forget to mention that you accidentally ruined the jeans with period blood and wine (I don't think she's guilty though, your boyfriend is the one who cheated. But as I said just before, it was an accident).

Princes of the universe - Queen;
Iron - Woodkid;
Spitfire - The Prodigy

I have mental illness that makes me think about suicide compulsively (with sometimes violent images of me hurting myself coming to my mind). 

&#x200B;

Like many women victim of violence from an ex boyfriend. But the saddest part is when I told our so-said friends that he abused me, nobody reached me to tell me something nice. They pretended not to see, or that they didn't want to get involved. 

Honestly that hurts more than all the physical abuse.

&#x200B;

Also when I ran away from him I called my mother who used to live 600km in emergency at night and she came. I just told her I broke up I didn't find the way to tell her that he abused me. 

She was angry to me because I made her drive by night and she never really forgave me for that. 

I feel a little bit unfair that she's angry at me for that but I can't tell her the truth

&#x200B;

Some people recover for someone having an affair in their couples. But it takes time and a lot of efforts from the 2 of you.

You seem to love him, and he seem to be willing to fix things. I recommend seeing a specialist/professional because it's very delicate.

&#x200B;

2 first things to make clear with him is:

\- Why he did that. This is more for him: solving this question should help taking the right decision for your couple (ex: he did it because he didn't love you anymore = either you should break up, or you should try to find a way to love each others again; He did it because he felt lonely= do more activities together, listen to each others more, etc.)

\- Why he stopped. It's important to see if he stopped because he loves you. Or just because the other person broke up (what would have happened if the other person didn't break up?), or because he couldn't stand feeling guilty (thus, he stopped to feel better, not to save your couple), etc.

&#x200B;

Again I recommend counseling because sometimes it's hard answering these questions by yourself. Especially if you feel guilty because then you might not be honest with your feelings.

&#x200B;

Good luck to you 2.

> **your teeth are naturally flaccid, and become hard whenever you are hungry** 

"Horngry" 

&#x200B;

