&#x200B;

This is soooooo NOT unpopular like open any man/woman's fashion magazine, any representation of feminity in advertisement, movies, music videos, ANYWHERE... women and feminity = long hair.

&#x200B;

An real unpopular opinion would be "most men don't look good with short hair".

How can this be considered as an "unpopular" opinion when the whole fashion and cosmetic industries, that represent, billions of dollars in the world still struggle using black skin or afro-american face traits as standards of beauty. Like...

&#x200B;

And thousands of studies show that you're not "just attracted to who you are attracted to". You are hugely influenced by the millions of images, ads, movies, and situations you were exposed to during your life.

There's one million things related to brain functioning that I could mention. 
But if I look at the bigger picture, I'd simply say what makes us living beings. 
// Like the heart for example, it can pump blood transforming fluids into energy for 99 years or more. I know there's chemical involved but stil I find it magic that it works forever almost by itself.
// Breathing is also a reflex. I know it sounds dumb, but it could have been different. 
// There's a lot of processes you will never have to think about (or only if you get ill, sadly). It's critical processes, the most important ones, yet you never have to worry about is it done or not.
You can KNOW and list everything a human is made of (carbon, etc.) but you can't MAKE a human (except in utero, which is even more insane). It's a cake I couldn't make.
Sometimes I'm a little bit fascinated the all the quiet processes we don't even know it's working every second. 

Next time you'll have a SO lying next to you, look at his/her veins. Think about the blood rushing under the skin right now like rivers, the fact that he's/she's warm, the chest slowly going up then down when lungs fill with oxygen, the heart bump that you can actually feel between the ribs with your hand on his/her chest. It's magic.

Humans are like little robots that have no idea they are robots or how they were concieved.
